{
  "description": "issue #738 (maintainer ruling): `clear_screen` under an EMPTY addressed set (`tell [ ]`) clears the shared surface and homes nobody. The two halves of the command follow different rules and this fixture separates them: the surface is shared, so it is cleared once however many turtles are addressed — including zero — and one `clear` event is emitted (spec/turtles-and-sprites.md:111); the homing is ordinary per-turtle movement applied once for each addressed turtle (:113), and there are none, so no `move`/`turn` is emitted at all. This is the same empty-set no-op every other per-turtle command already has (shape-empty-set-no-op). The main turtle is deliberately never addressed here — `tell [ ]` narrows the set to nothing rather than to it — and the trailing `print pos`/`print heading` prove it was NOT homed: it is still at [0 10] heading 30, where the `forward`/`right` left it. Before the ruling this program homed the main turtle and stamped the `clear` with its id, because the homing followed the CURRENT turtle (which `tell [ ]` leaves pointing at the main turtle) rather than the addressed set. That is exactly the spurious home a consumer would also produce by routing an un-stamped `clear` to a default turtle, which :113 now forbids: \"consumers MUST NOT read a `clear` event as an instruction to move a turtle\".",
